rsed H28 main-effect lead. I also close H12 and H22 because the feature_045/outcome_012 evidence is below or not clearly above the relevant threshold and not sufficiently precise.\n\nThe calibration controls mostly reinforce that many measured binary predictors have wide intervals and small central effects. H29 for feature_060 on outcome_006 had a point estimate similar to the feature_002/outcome_006 overall estimate, 0.0194, but interval -0.015 to 0.054; this makes feature_002 less distinctive, so H29 remains unresolved and closed. H40 for feature_052/outcome_012 was opposite and below threshold, reject and close. H39 for feature_065/outcome_006 and H41 for feature_020/outcome_014 had near-null central estimates though intervals are compatible with larger values; I leave them unresolved but closed because they are not compelling follow-up targets.\n\nThe strongest new result is H38: feature_042 on outcome_014. Its signed estimate is 0.0351, about 3.8 times the outcome_014 10%-scale reference of 0.0093, and its 95% interval is -0.0012 to 0.0714. This is the only new result whose central estimate is clearly clinically meaningful and whose interval is only marginally compatible with no effect. However, it was registered as a calibration control, not a prior hypothesis, and it emerged as the strongest among multiple comparisons. Its lower bound still touches zero, so immediate acceptance would be too aggressive. I therefore leave H38 unresolved but active and request independent validation for H38. Validation will not prove causality or generalizability; it will only test whether this dataset-specific association is reproducible on independent data under the multiplicity-adjusted validation interval. If it does not replicate, the feature_042 lead should be closed. If it does, follow-up will test whether the effect is stable across feature_018 and feature_009 strata and whether it is outcome-specific.",
